From 7d5a51dd889fa48ea6813af841c1cf02a71c2618 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Reed <reedswork@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 14:29:30 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 56/56] client: real ECH from the host's HTTPS record, retried
 as Chrome does

Chrome reads a name's HTTPS DNS record next to its addresses and, when the
record carries an ech config list, encrypts its ClientHello to it (RFC
9849): crypto.cloudflare.com/cdn-cgi/trace reads sni=encrypted, tls-ech.dev
"You are using ECH". Epoxy only ever sent the GREASE ECH of the Chrome hello.

EpoxyClientOptions takes an ech_resolver, `(host) => Promise<Uint8Array |
null>`, asked for a DNS name's ECHConfigList while the wisp stream to it
opens (an address is never asked). With a list rustls can use (the X25519
suites of rustls's ring HPKE), the HTTP and WebSocket connection offers
real ECH through ClientConfig::set_ech_mode, with Chrome's outer and inner
hello. A server that rejects the offer and sends retry configs gets one
more connection with those; one that rejects without them (ECH is off
there, or it answered with TLS 1.2) gets one without ECH, as Chrome
reconnects. Raw connect_tls streams offer no ECH.
